**Pattern:** DFS (recursive)

**Key insight:** Swap children at each node, then recurse into both; order (pre/post) does not matter.

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode invertTree(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return null;
        TreeNode left = invertTree(root.left);
        root.left = invertTree(root.right);
        root.right = left;
        return root;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** Overwriting `root.left` before saving it loses the original subtree.

**Pattern:** DFS (post-order)

**Key insight:** Depth of a node = 1 + max depth of its children; null has depth 0.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return 0;
        return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(root.left), maxDepth(root.right));
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** Returning 1 for null double-counts every leaf level.

**Pattern:** DFS returning height + global best

**Key insight:** At each node, the longest path through it is leftHeight + rightHeight; return height upward, track the max on the side.

```java
class Solution {
    private int best = 0;
    public int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode root) {
        height(root);
        return best;
    }
    private int height(TreeNode n) {
        if (n == null) return 0;
        int l = height(n.left), r = height(n.right);
        best = Math.max(best, l + r);
        return 1 + Math.max(l, r);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** The longest path need not pass through the root, so check every node.

**Pattern:** DFS with sentinel height

**Key insight:** Compute height bottom-up and return -1 as soon as any subtree is unbalanced, so each node is visited once.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isBalanced(TreeNode root) {
        return h(root) != -1;
    }
    private int h(TreeNode n) {
        if (n == null) return 0;
        int l = h(n.left);
        if (l == -1) return -1;
        int r = h(n.right);
        if (r == -1 || Math.abs(l - r) > 1) return -1;
        return 1 + Math.max(l, r);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** Calling a separate height() at every node makes it O(n²).

**Pattern:** Parallel DFS

**Key insight:** Both null → true; one null or values differ → false; otherwise recurse on both pairs of children.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        if (p == null || q == null) return p == q;
        return p.val == q.val && isSameTree(p.left, q.left) && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** Checking only values without checking structure (null vs non-null) gives false positives.

**Pattern:** DFS + Same Tree check

**Key insight:** Try a same-tree comparison at every node of the big tree.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSubtree(TreeNode root, TreeNode subRoot) {
        if (root == null) return false;
        if (same(root, subRoot)) return true;
        return isSubtree(root.left, subRoot) || isSubtree(root.right, subRoot);
    }
    private boolean same(TreeNode a, TreeNode b) {
        if (a == null || b == null) return a == b;
        return a.val == b.val && same(a.left, b.left) && same(a.right, b.right);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** A matching value is not enough — the whole subtree below must match, including where it ends.

**Pattern:** BST walk

**Key insight:** Walk down from the root: if both values are smaller go left, both larger go right, otherwise this is the split point.

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        TreeNode cur = root;
        while (cur != null) {
            if (p.val < cur.val && q.val < cur.val) cur = cur.left;
            else if (p.val > cur.val && q.val > cur.val) cur = cur.right;
            else return cur;
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(h) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Ignoring the BST property and doing a generic LCA search wastes the ordering.

**Pattern:** BFS by level

**Key insight:** Snapshot the queue size at the start of each round; that many pops make up one level.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        if (root != null) q.add(root);
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {
                TreeNode n = q.poll();
                level.add(n.val);
                if (n.left != null) q.add(n.left);
                if (n.right != null) q.add(n.right);
            }
            res.add(level);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(w)

**Pitfall:** Using `q.size()` as the live loop bound changes as children are added.

**Pattern:** BFS by level (or right-first DFS)

**Key insight:** The last node popped in each BFS level is the one visible from the right.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        if (root != null) q.add(root);
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int size = q.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                TreeNode n = q.poll();
                if (i == size - 1) res.add(n.val);
                if (n.left != null) q.add(n.left);
                if (n.right != null) q.add(n.right);
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(w)

**Pitfall:** Only following right children misses left nodes that stick out deeper.

**Pattern:** DFS carrying path max

**Key insight:** Pass down the max seen so far; a node is good if its value ≥ that max.

```java
class Solution {
    public int goodNodes(TreeNode root) {
        return dfs(root, Integer.MIN_VALUE);
    }
    private int dfs(TreeNode n, int maxSoFar) {
        if (n == null) return 0;
        int good = n.val >= maxSoFar ? 1 : 0;
        int m = Math.max(maxSoFar, n.val);
        return good + dfs(n.left, m) + dfs(n.right, m);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** Use `>=`, not `>` — equal values still count as good.

**Pattern:** DFS with bounds

**Key insight:** Pass an open (low, high) range down; going left tightens high, going right tightens low.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
        return ok(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);
    }
    private boolean ok(TreeNode n, long lo, long hi) {
        if (n == null) return true;
        if (n.val <= lo || n.val >= hi) return false;
        return ok(n.left, lo, n.val) && ok(n.right, n.val, hi);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** Comparing only to the direct parent misses violations from grandparents; also use long bounds for Integer.MIN/MAX values.

**Pattern:** Iterative in-order traversal

**Key insight:** In-order visits a BST in sorted order; stop at the k-th visit.

```java
class Solution {
    public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {
        Deque<TreeNode> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
        TreeNode cur = root;
        while (cur != null || !st.isEmpty()) {
            while (cur != null) { st.push(cur); cur = cur.left; }
            cur = st.pop();
            if (--k == 0) return cur.val;
            cur = cur.right;
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(h + k) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** Collecting the whole traversal into a list works but wastes O(n) when k is small.

**Pattern:** Divide & conquer + index map

**Key insight:** Preorder's next item is the root; its position in inorder splits left and right subtree sizes.

```java
class Solution {
    private Map<Integer, Integer> pos = new HashMap<>();
    private int pre = 0;
    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {
        for (int i = 0; i < inorder.length; i++) pos.put(inorder[i], i);
        return build(preorder, 0, inorder.length - 1);
    }
    private TreeNode build(int[] preorder, int lo, int hi) {
        if (lo > hi) return null;
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[pre++]);
        int mid = pos.get(root.val);
        root.left = build(preorder, lo, mid - 1);
        root.right = build(preorder, mid + 1, hi);
        return root;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Build the left subtree before the right, since the preorder pointer is consumed in that order.

**Pattern:** DFS returning best downward gain

**Key insight:** Each node returns its best single-branch gain (negatives clipped to 0); the path bending through it is val + leftGain + rightGain.

```java
class Solution {
    private int best = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    public int maxPathSum(TreeNode root) {
        gain(root);
        return best;
    }
    private int gain(TreeNode n) {
        if (n == null) return 0;
        int l = Math.max(0, gain(n.left));
        int r = Math.max(0, gain(n.right));
        best = Math.max(best, n.val + l + r);
        return n.val + Math.max(l, r);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(h)

**Pitfall:** Initializing best to 0 fails when every value is negative.

**Pattern:** Preorder DFS with null markers

**Key insight:** Preorder plus an explicit marker for nulls is enough to rebuild the tree unambiguously.

```java
class Codec {
    public String serialize(TreeNode root) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        write(root, sb);
        return sb.toString();
    }
    private void write(TreeNode n, StringBuilder sb) {
        if (n == null) { sb.append("#,"); return; }
        sb.append(n.val).append(',');
        write(n.left, sb);
        write(n.right, sb);
    }
    public TreeNode deserialize(String data) {
        Deque<String> q = new ArrayDeque<>(Arrays.asList(data.split(",")));
        return read(q);
    }
    private TreeNode read(Deque<String> q) {
        String s = q.poll();
        if (s.equals("#")) return null;
        TreeNode n = new TreeNode(Integer.parseInt(s));
        n.left = read(q);
        n.right = read(q);
        return n;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Without null markers, preorder alone cannot distinguish different tree shapes.

**Pattern:** Trie

**Key insight:** Each node holds 26 child slots and an end-of-word flag; walk character by character.

```java
class Trie {
    private final Trie[] next = new Trie[26];
    private boolean end;
    public void insert(String word) {
        Trie n = this;
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
            if (n.next[c - 'a'] == null) n.next[c - 'a'] = new Trie();
            n = n.next[c - 'a'];
        }
        n.end = true;
    }
    public boolean search(String word) {
        Trie n = walk(word);
        return n != null && n.end;
    }
    public boolean startsWith(String prefix) {
        return walk(prefix) != null;
    }
    private Trie walk(String s) {
        Trie n = this;
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            n = n.next[c - 'a'];
            if (n == null) return null;
        }
        return n;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(L) per op / O(total chars)

**Pitfall:** `search` must check the end flag — reaching the node only proves a prefix exists.

**Pattern:** Trie + DFS for wildcards

**Key insight:** Normal letters follow one child; a `.` branches into every non-null child.

```java
class WordDictionary {
    private final WordDictionary[] next = new WordDictionary[26];
    private boolean end;
    public void addWord(String word) {
        WordDictionary n = this;
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
            if (n.next[c - 'a'] == null) n.next[c - 'a'] = new WordDictionary();
            n = n.next[c - 'a'];
        }
        n.end = true;
    }
    public boolean search(String word) {
        return find(word, 0, this);
    }
    private boolean find(String w, int i, WordDictionary n) {
        if (i == w.length()) return n.end;
        char c = w.charAt(i);
        if (c != '.') {
            WordDictionary child = n.next[c - 'a'];
            return child != null && find(w, i + 1, child);
        }
        for (WordDictionary child : n.next)
            if (child != null && find(w, i + 1, child)) return true;
        return false;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** add O(L); search O(26^dots · L) worst / O(total chars)

**Pitfall:** Returning on the first wildcard branch instead of trying all children.

**Pattern:** Trie + grid backtracking

**Key insight:** Put all words in a trie and DFS from each cell, following the trie so dead prefixes are pruned immediately.

```java
class Solution {
    static class Node { Node[] k = new Node[26]; String word; }
    public List<String> findWords(char[][] board, String[] words) {
        Node root = new Node();
        for (String w : words) {
            Node n = root;
            for (char c : w.toCharArray()) n = n.k[c - 'a'] == null ? (n.k[c - 'a'] = new Node()) : n.k[c - 'a'];
            n.word = w;
        }
        List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int r = 0; r < board.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < board[0].length; c++) dfs(board, r, c, root, res);
        return res;
    }
    private void dfs(char[][] b, int r, int c, Node parent, List<String> res) {
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= b.length || c >= b[0].length || b[r][c] == '#') return;
        char ch = b[r][c];
        Node n = parent.k[ch - 'a'];
        if (n == null) return;
        if (n.word != null) { res.add(n.word); n.word = null; }
        b[r][c] = '#';
        dfs(b, r + 1, c, n, res); dfs(b, r - 1, c, n, res);
        dfs(b, r, c + 1, n, res); dfs(b, r, c - 1, n, res);
        b[r][c] = ch;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(R·C·4·3^(L-1)) / O(total chars)

**Pitfall:** Not clearing `word` after finding it adds duplicates to the result.

**Pattern:** Min-heap of size k

**Key insight:** Keep only the k largest values in a min-heap; its top is the answer.

```java
class KthLargest {
    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();
    private final int k;
    public KthLargest(int k, int[] nums) {
        this.k = k;
        for (int x : nums) add(x);
    }
    public int add(int val) {
        heap.offer(val);
        if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
        return heap.peek();
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(log k) per add / O(k)

**Pitfall:** Using a max-heap forces you to pop k items per query.

**Pattern:** Max-heap simulation

**Key insight:** A max-heap gives the two heaviest stones in O(log n) each round.

```java
class Solution {
    public int lastStoneWeight(int[] stones) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
        for (int s : stones) pq.offer(s);
        while (pq.size() > 1) {
            int a = pq.poll(), b = pq.poll();
            if (a != b) pq.offer(a - b);
        }
        return pq.isEmpty() ? 0 : pq.peek();
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n log n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Forgetting the empty-heap case when all stones cancel out.

**Pattern:** Max-heap of size k

**Key insight:** Keep a max-heap by squared distance; evict the farthest once it exceeds k.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] kClosest(int[][] points, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> Integer.compare(b[0] * b[0] + b[1] * b[1], a[0] * a[0] + a[1] * a[1]));
        for (int[] p : points) {
            pq.offer(p);
            if (pq.size() > k) pq.poll();
        }
        return pq.toArray(new int[0][]);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n log k) / O(k)

**Pitfall:** Taking sqrt is unnecessary and introduces floating-point issues; compare squared distances.

**Pattern:** Min-heap of size k (or Quickselect)

**Key insight:** After streaming all values through a size-k min-heap, the top is the k-th largest.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findKthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
        for (int x : nums) {
            pq.offer(x);
            if (pq.size() > k) pq.poll();
        }
        return pq.peek();
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n log k) / O(k)

**Pitfall:** Deduplicating values — the k-th largest counts duplicates separately.

**Pattern:** Greedy counting (or max-heap + cooldown queue)

**Key insight:** The most frequent task forms (maxCount-1) frames of length n+1, plus one slot per task tied for max; the answer is at least the task count.

```java
class Solution {
    public int leastInterval(char[] tasks, int n) {
        int[] cnt = new int[26];
        for (char t : tasks) cnt[t - 'A']++;
        int max = 0, tied = 0;
        for (int c : cnt) {
            if (c > max) { max = c; tied = 1; }
            else if (c == max) tied++;
        }
        return Math.max(tasks.length, (max - 1) * (n + 1) + tied);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Forgetting the `max(tasks.length, ...)` — with many distinct tasks there are no idles.

**Pattern:** K-way merge with a heap

**Key insight:** Store each user's tweets newest-last with a global timestamp; merge followees' lists via a max-heap by time, stopping at 10.

```java
class Twitter {
    private int time = 0;
    private final Map<Integer, List<int[]>> tweets = new HashMap<>();
    private final Map<Integer, Set<Integer>> follows = new HashMap<>();
    public void postTweet(int userId, int tweetId) {
        tweets.computeIfAbsent(userId, x -> new ArrayList<>()).add(new int[]{time++, tweetId});
    }
    public List<Integer> getNewsFeed(int userId) {
        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b[0] - a[0]); // {time, tweetId, user, idx}
        Set<Integer> users = new HashSet<>(follows.getOrDefault(userId, Set.of()));
        users.add(userId);
        for (int u : users) {
            List<int[]> t = tweets.get(u);
            if (t != null && !t.isEmpty()) { int i = t.size() - 1; pq.offer(new int[]{t.get(i)[0], t.get(i)[1], u, i}); }
        }
        List<Integer> feed = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!pq.isEmpty() && feed.size() < 10) {
            int[] top = pq.poll();
            feed.add(top[1]);
            int i = top[3] - 1; if (i >= 0) { int[] t = tweets.get(top[2]).get(i); pq.offer(new int[]{t[0], t[1], top[2], i}); }
        }
        return feed;
    }
    public void follow(int followerId, int followeeId) {
        if (followerId != followeeId) follows.computeIfAbsent(followerId, x -> new HashSet<>()).add(followeeId);
    }
    public void unfollow(int followerId, int followeeId) {
        follows.getOrDefault(followerId, new HashSet<>()).remove(followeeId);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** feed O(F + 10 log F) / O(users + tweets)

**Pitfall:** Self-follow/unfollow edge cases: a user must always see their own tweets.

**Pattern:** Two heaps

**Key insight:** A max-heap holds the smaller half and a min-heap the larger half; keep sizes within one so the median sits at the tops.

```java
class MedianFinder {
    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> low = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> high = new PriorityQueue<>();
    public void addNum(int num) {
        low.offer(num);
        high.offer(low.poll());
        if (high.size() > low.size()) low.offer(high.poll());
    }
    public double findMedian() {
        if (low.size() > high.size()) return low.peek();
        return (low.peek() + (long) high.peek()) / 2.0;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** add O(log n), median O(1) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Pushing straight into one heap without routing through the other breaks the "low ≤ high" invariant.

**Pattern:** Backtracking (include / skip)

**Key insight:** At each index branch on taking or not taking the element; record the path at every node.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        go(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }
    private void go(int[] nums, int start, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        res.add(new ArrayList<>(path));
        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
            path.add(nums[i]);
            go(nums, i + 1, path, res);
            path.remove(path.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n·2ⁿ) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Adding `path` itself instead of a copy — every entry ends up as the same (empty) list.

**Pattern:** Backtracking with reuse

**Key insight:** Recurse with the same start index to allow reuse; sort and break once a candidate overshoots.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum(int[] candidates, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(candidates);
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        go(candidates, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }
    private void go(int[] c, int start, int left, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (left == 0) { res.add(new ArrayList<>(path)); return; }
        for (int i = start; i < c.length && c[i] <= left; i++) {
            path.add(c[i]);
            go(c, i, left - c[i], path, res);
            path.remove(path.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n^(t/min)) / O(t/min)

**Pitfall:** Restarting from index 0 each call produces permutations of the same combination.

**Pattern:** Backtracking with duplicate skipping

**Key insight:** Sort, move to i+1 after picking, and skip equal values at the same depth (`i > start && c[i] == c[i-1]`).

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(candidates);
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        go(candidates, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }
    private void go(int[] c, int start, int left, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (left == 0) { res.add(new ArrayList<>(path)); return; }
        for (int i = start; i < c.length && c[i] <= left; i++) {
            if (i > start && c[i] == c[i - 1]) continue;
            path.add(c[i]);
            go(c, i + 1, left - c[i], path, res);
            path.remove(path.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(2ⁿ) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Skipping with `i > 0` instead of `i > start` wrongly forbids using two equal values in one combo.

**Pattern:** Backtracking with used[]

**Key insight:** Build the path one slot at a time from any unused element; record when the path is full.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        go(nums, new boolean[nums.length], new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }
    private void go(int[] nums, boolean[] used, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (path.size() == nums.length) { res.add(new ArrayList<>(path)); return; }
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (used[i]) continue;
            used[i] = true; path.add(nums[i]);
            go(nums, used, path, res);
            used[i] = false; path.remove(path.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n·n!) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Forgetting to reset `used[i]` on the way back out.

**Pattern:** Backtracking with duplicate skipping

**Key insight:** Sort first; at each depth only branch on the first of a run of equal values.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        go(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }
    private void go(int[] nums, int start, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        res.add(new ArrayList<>(path));
        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;
            path.add(nums[i]);
            go(nums, i + 1, path, res);
            path.remove(path.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n·2ⁿ) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Skipping duplicates without sorting first leaves non-adjacent duplicates undetected.

**Pattern:** Grid DFS backtracking

**Key insight:** From each matching start cell, DFS on the next character, temporarily marking the cell as visited.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean exist(char[][] board, String word) {
        for (int r = 0; r < board.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < board[0].length; c++)
                if (dfs(board, word, 0, r, c)) return true;
        return false;
    }
    private boolean dfs(char[][] b, String w, int i, int r, int c) {
        if (i == w.length()) return true;
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= b.length || c >= b[0].length || b[r][c] != w.charAt(i)) return false;
        char saved = b[r][c];
        b[r][c] = '#';
        boolean found = dfs(b, w, i + 1, r + 1, c) || dfs(b, w, i + 1, r - 1, c)
                     || dfs(b, w, i + 1, r, c + 1) || dfs(b, w, i + 1, r, c - 1);
        b[r][c] = saved;
        return found;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(R·C·3^L) / O(L)

**Pitfall:** Not restoring the cell after exploring corrupts later searches from other start cells.

**Pattern:** Backtracking over cut positions

**Key insight:** From the current start, try every end that makes a palindrome prefix, then recurse on the rest.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> partition(String s) {
        List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        go(s, 0, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }
    private void go(String s, int start, List<String> path, List<List<String>> res) {
        if (start == s.length()) { res.add(new ArrayList<>(path)); return; }
        for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {
            if (!isPal(s, start, end)) continue;
            path.add(s.substring(start, end + 1));
            go(s, end + 1, path, res);
            path.remove(path.size() - 1);
        }
    }
    private boolean isPal(String s, int i, int j) {
        while (i < j) if (s.charAt(i++) != s.charAt(j--)) return false;
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n·2ⁿ) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Off-by-one with substring's exclusive end index.

**Pattern:** Backtracking (Cartesian product)

**Key insight:** Each digit position picks one of its mapped letters; recurse to the next digit.

```java
class Solution {
    private static final String[] MAP = {"", "", "abc", "def", "ghi", "jkl", "mno", "pqrs", "tuv", "wxyz"};
    public List<String> letterCombinations(String digits) {
        List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();
        if (!digits.isEmpty()) go(digits, 0, new StringBuilder(), res);
        return res;
    }
    private void go(String d, int i, StringBuilder sb, List<String> res) {
        if (i == d.length()) { res.add(sb.toString()); return; }
        for (char c : MAP[d.charAt(i) - '0'].toCharArray()) {
            sb.append(c);
            go(d, i + 1, sb, res);
            sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n·4ⁿ) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Empty input must return `[]`, not `[""]`.

**Pattern:** Row-by-row backtracking with sets

**Key insight:** Place one queen per row; track used columns and both diagonals (r+c and r-c) for O(1) conflict checks.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        go(0, n, new int[n], new boolean[n], new boolean[2 * n], new boolean[2 * n], res);
        return res;
    }
    private void go(int r, int n, int[] q, boolean[] col, boolean[] d1, boolean[] d2, List<List<String>> res) {
        if (r == n) {
            List<String> board = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int c : q) { char[] row = new char[n]; Arrays.fill(row, '.'); row[c] = 'Q'; board.add(new String(row)); }
            res.add(board);
            return;
        }
        for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {
            if (col[c] || d1[r + c] || d2[r - c + n]) continue;
            q[r] = c; col[c] = d1[r + c] = d2[r - c + n] = true;
            go(r + 1, n, q, col, d1, d2, res);
            col[c] = d1[r + c] = d2[r - c + n] = false;
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n!) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** `r - c` can be negative — offset it by n before indexing.

**Pattern:** Grid DFS flood fill

**Key insight:** Scan cells; each unvisited land cell starts a new island and a flood fill sinks the whole island.

```java
class Solution {
    public int numIslands(char[][] grid) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < grid[0].length; c++)
                if (grid[r][c] == '1') { count++; sink(grid, r, c); }
        return count;
    }
    private void sink(char[][] g, int r, int c) {
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= g.length || c >= g[0].length || g[r][c] != '1') return;
        g[r][c] = '0';
        sink(g, r + 1, c); sink(g, r - 1, c); sink(g, r, c + 1); sink(g, r, c - 1);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(R·C) / O(R·C)

**Pitfall:** Marking visited after recursing (instead of before) causes infinite recursion.

**Pattern:** Grid DFS returning size

**Key insight:** Flood fill returns 1 + sizes of its four neighbors; take the max over all starts.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxAreaOfIsland(int[][] grid) {
        int best = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < grid[0].length; c++)
                best = Math.max(best, area(grid, r, c));
        return best;
    }
    private int area(int[][] g, int r, int c) {
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= g.length || c >= g[0].length || g[r][c] != 1) return 0;
        g[r][c] = 0;
        return 1 + area(g, r + 1, c) + area(g, r - 1, c) + area(g, r, c + 1) + area(g, r, c - 1);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(R·C) / O(R·C)

**Pitfall:** Diagonal cells are not connected — only four directions count.

**Pattern:** DFS + old→new map

**Key insight:** Map each original node to its clone before visiting neighbors, so cycles reuse the existing clone.

```java
class Solution {
    private final Map<Node, Node> copies = new HashMap<>();
    public Node cloneGraph(Node node) {
        if (node == null) return null;
        if (copies.containsKey(node)) return copies.get(node);
        Node copy = new Node(node.val);
        copies.put(node, copy);
        for (Node nb : node.neighbors) copy.neighbors.add(cloneGraph(nb));
        return copy;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(V+E) / O(V)

**Pitfall:** Inserting into the map after recursing loops forever on cycles.

**Pattern:** Multi-source BFS

**Key insight:** Start BFS from all gates at once; the first time a room is reached is its shortest distance.

```java
class Solution {
    public void wallsAndGates(int[][] rooms) {
        int R = rooms.length, C = rooms[0].length, INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        Deque<int[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)
                if (rooms[r][c] == 0) q.add(new int[]{r, c});
        int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int[] p = q.poll();
            for (int[] d : dirs) {
                int r = p[0] + d[0], c = p[1] + d[1];
                if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= R || c >= C || rooms[r][c] != INF) continue;
                rooms[r][c] = rooms[p[0]][p[1]] + 1;
                q.add(new int[]{r, c});
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(R·C) / O(R·C)

**Pitfall:** BFS from each gate separately is O((R·C)²); seed all gates together.

**Pattern:** Multi-source BFS by level

**Key insight:** Seed the queue with all rotten oranges and count fresh ones; each BFS level is one minute.

```java
class Solution {
    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {
        Deque<int[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        int fresh = 0, minutes = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < grid[0].length; c++) {
                if (grid[r][c] == 2) q.add(new int[]{r, c});
                else if (grid[r][c] == 1) fresh++;
            }
        int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};
        while (!q.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {
            for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {
                int[] p = q.poll();
                for (int[] d : dirs) {
                    int r = p[0] + d[0], c = p[1] + d[1];
                    if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= grid.length || c >= grid[0].length || grid[r][c] != 1) continue;
                    grid[r][c] = 2; fresh--;
                    q.add(new int[]{r, c});
                }
            }
            minutes++;
        }
        return fresh == 0 ? minutes : -1;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(R·C) / O(R·C)

**Pitfall:** Counting a minute for the final empty level overstates the answer by one — stop when fresh hits 0.

**Pattern:** Reverse DFS from each ocean

**Key insight:** Flow uphill from each ocean's border cells to mark what can reach it; answer is the intersection.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> pacificAtlantic(int[][] h) {
        int R = h.length, C = h[0].length;
        boolean[][] pac = new boolean[R][C], atl = new boolean[R][C];
        for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) { dfs(h, pac, r, 0, 0); dfs(h, atl, r, C - 1, 0); }
        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) { dfs(h, pac, 0, c, 0); dfs(h, atl, R - 1, c, 0); }
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)
                if (pac[r][c] && atl[r][c]) res.add(List.of(r, c));
        return res;
    }
    private void dfs(int[][] h, boolean[][] seen, int r, int c, int prev) {
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= h.length || c >= h[0].length || seen[r][c] || h[r][c] < prev) return;
        seen[r][c] = true;
        dfs(h, seen, r + 1, c, h[r][c]); dfs(h, seen, r - 1, c, h[r][c]);
        dfs(h, seen, r, c + 1, h[r][c]); dfs(h, seen, r, c - 1, h[r][c]);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(R·C) / O(R·C)

**Pitfall:** Running a forward search from every cell is O((R·C)²); search backwards from the oceans.

**Pattern:** Border DFS then sweep

**Key insight:** Mark Os reachable from the border as safe; then flip remaining Os to X and restore safe ones.

```java
class Solution {
    public void solve(char[][] b) {
        int R = b.length, C = b[0].length;
        for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) { mark(b, r, 0); mark(b, r, C - 1); }
        for (int c = 0; c < C; c++) { mark(b, 0, c); mark(b, R - 1, c); }
        for (int r = 0; r < R; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < C; c++)
                b[r][c] = b[r][c] == 'S' ? 'O' : 'X';
    }
    private void mark(char[][] b, int r, int c) {
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= b.length || c >= b[0].length || b[r][c] != 'O') return;
        b[r][c] = 'S';
        mark(b, r + 1, c); mark(b, r - 1, c); mark(b, r, c + 1); mark(b, r, c - 1);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(R·C) / O(R·C)

**Pitfall:** Trying to detect "surrounded" from the inside is error-prone; start from the border instead.

**Pattern:** Topological sort (Kahn's BFS)

**Key insight:** Repeatedly take courses with zero remaining prerequisites; if all get taken there is no cycle.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean canFinish(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {
        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        int[] indeg = new int[numCourses];
        for (int[] p : prerequisites) { adj.get(p[1]).add(p[0]); indeg[p[0]]++; }
        Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) if (indeg[i] == 0) q.add(i);
        int taken = 0;
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int c = q.poll();
            taken++;
            for (int nx : adj.get(c)) if (--indeg[nx] == 0) q.add(nx);
        }
        return taken == numCourses;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(V+E) / O(V+E)

**Pitfall:** Edge direction: `[a, b]` means b must come before a, so the edge is b → a.

**Pattern:** Topological sort (Kahn's BFS)

**Key insight:** Record courses in the order they reach indegree 0; fewer than n recorded means a cycle.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] findOrder(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {
        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        int[] indeg = new int[numCourses];
        for (int[] p : prerequisites) { adj.get(p[1]).add(p[0]); indeg[p[0]]++; }
        Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) if (indeg[i] == 0) q.add(i);
        int[] order = new int[numCourses];
        int k = 0;
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int c = q.poll();
            order[k++] = c;
            for (int nx : adj.get(c)) if (--indeg[nx] == 0) q.add(nx);
        }
        return k == numCourses ? order : new int[0];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(V+E) / O(V+E)

**Pitfall:** Returning a partial order when a cycle exists — must return an empty array.

**Pattern:** Union-Find

**Key insight:** A tree has exactly n-1 edges and no edge joins two already-connected nodes.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean validTree(int n, int[][] edges) {
        if (edges.length != n - 1) return false;
        int[] parent = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
        for (int[] e : edges) {
            int a = find(parent, e[0]), b = find(parent, e[1]);
            if (a == b) return false;
            parent[a] = b;
        }
        return true;
    }
    private int find(int[] p, int x) {
        while (p[x] != x) { p[x] = p[p[x]]; x = p[x]; }
        return x;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(E·α(n)) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Checking only "no cycle" misses disconnected forests; the n-1 edge count covers that.

**Pattern:** Union-Find

**Key insight:** Start with n components and subtract one for every edge that merges two different roots.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countComponents(int n, int[][] edges) {
        int[] parent = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
        int comps = n;
        for (int[] e : edges) {
            int a = find(parent, e[0]), b = find(parent, e[1]);
            if (a != b) { parent[a] = b; comps--; }
        }
        return comps;
    }
    private int find(int[] p, int x) {
        while (p[x] != x) { p[x] = p[p[x]]; x = p[x]; }
        return x;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(E·α(n)) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Linking `e[0]` to `e[1]` directly instead of their roots breaks the structure.

**Pattern:** Union-Find

**Key insight:** Process edges in order; the first edge whose endpoints already share a root closes the cycle.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] findRedundantConnection(int[][] edges) {
        int[] parent = new int[edges.length + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < parent.length; i++) parent[i] = i;
        for (int[] e : edges) {
            int a = find(parent, e[0]), b = find(parent, e[1]);
            if (a == b) return e;
            parent[a] = b;
        }
        return new int[0];
    }
    private int find(int[] p, int x) {
        while (p[x] != x) { p[x] = p[p[x]]; x = p[x]; }
        return x;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n·α(n)) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Nodes are 1-indexed, so size the parent array n+1.

**Pattern:** BFS over implicit graph

**Key insight:** Each word is a node; neighbors are dictionary words one letter away. BFS gives the shortest chain.

```java
class Solution {
    public int ladderLength(String beginWord, String endWord, List<String> wordList) {
        Set<String> dict = new HashSet<>(wordList);
        if (!dict.contains(endWord)) return 0;
        Deque<String> q = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(beginWord));
        dict.remove(beginWord);
        for (int steps = 1; !q.isEmpty(); steps++) {
            for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {
                char[] w = q.poll().toCharArray();
                for (int i = 0; i < w.length; i++) {
                    char orig = w[i];
                    for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {
                        w[i] = c;
                        String nx = new String(w);
                        if (nx.equals(endWord)) return steps + 1;
                        if (dict.remove(nx)) q.add(nx);
                    }
                    w[i] = orig;
                }
            }
        }
        return 0;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(N·L·26) / O(N)

**Pitfall:** Not removing words when enqueued lets BFS revisit them and blow up.
